 but it will  give  you know tools that we need  in order to provide affordable housing  funding which  which i'm sure you know affordable  housing is a huge issue here  whether in the south or in the north  indeed it is that was our christian  cazares interviewing assemblywoman  elaine marzola  you can check out our interviews with  all three new members of the hispanic  legislative caucus  over at eight newsnow dot com now you  may have heard  activists or politicians using the term  recently latin  x now the x replaces the masculine  latino or the feminine latina  makes the term gender neutral as our  hector mejia explains while the movement  to use latinx is gaining traction around  the world  very few hispanics here in the u.s  actually use it  i actually had to look it up i was also  kind of like whoa  why is there an x at the end the greater  population remains divided  and so the x is the start but it's not  enough  the pew research center points out the  gender neutral term latinx is being  embraced by celebrities politicians and  grassroots organizations  latinx folks are going to be the biggest  voting block in this election  in 2020 apple even created a latinx  heritage month section with videos  playlists and other content not only  does our community  still struggle with internalized racism  it also struggles with internalized  queer phobia and sexism a study by the  pew research center also found out  that most latino adults 76 percent have  not even heard of the term  while one in four u.s hispanics have  only three percent actually use it  dr erica abad is a professor at unlv  where more than 25 percent of the  students are latino or latinx  student life often uses it to promote  their events  frederick aldama has written more than  45 books on the topic  plus a documentary on latinx superheroes  we're sort of in  in a movement as opposed to a series of  moments of representation  in the media the ohio state university  professor says  what's important here is that the  community is the one behind the movement  we've all been told by the census by the  government  you know how to self-identify and how to  call ourselves we don't need to be  prescriptive about it like telling  people you need to be  self-identifying latinx this is really  about giving us options the transgender  non-binary community experiences too  much violence on a daily basis i don't  want to see that anymore  and sometimes it's and something as  simple as recognizing  their gender pronouns their name and the  terms  they prefer makes a world of difference  hector mejia 8 news now  now that pew research center study that  we referenced there it found  young hispanic women are among the most  likely to use the term  latin x we have a link to the study and  more information over at eight newsnow  dot com  next on politics now ready to run  senator catherine cortez masto announces  her re-election campaign  the case she's making and how much money  her campaign is going to be starting  with we have that coming up  plus washington dc bureau correspondent  alexander limon  we'll talk about the nominee for the  secretary of the interior and her ties  to the west  it's 8 news now you're watching  politics now  this week nevada democratic senator  catherine cortez masto announced that  she is running for reelection  just this last year i was able to pass  13 pieces of legislation  and i did that because i reached across  the aisle and worked with a republican  colleague  and we got it done in a republican  administration  so that that tells you kind of gives you  an idea the focus here is is to really  solving problems for people  in my state and that that's not going to  change  senator cortez masto is up for  re-election in 2022  she's the first latina to serve in the  u.s senate cortes masto chairs a  subcommittee on the senate energy and  natural resources committee  and that committee oversees public lands  in the mining industry  cortes masto's fec filings show her  campaign started 2021 with more than 3  million dollars on hand in cash  so far no republicans have formally  declared that they're going to run  against her  another nevada democrat has snagged some  key assignments in congress we're  talking about representative dina titus  she is the longest serving member of the  nevada delegation in washington  she'll chair the gaming caucus and the  tourism caucus  the travel and tourism caucus has about  a hundred members  the gaming caucus about 30. president  joe biden's cabinet nominees are now  going through their confirmation  hearings in washington  one position with a big influence here  in nevada is is the secretary of the  interior  new mexico congresswoman deb holland  started her confirmation hearings for  that position this week  the government agencies under the  department of interior control public  lands which as you know make up more  than 85 percent of nevada  new mexico congresswoman deb holland is  an historic nominee for  secretary of the interior she's a native  american woman but  her confirmation hearings have been  pretty tough one of the tougher ones for  the biden administration we're joined by  alexander limon to talk about  some of the hang-ups that senators had  about congresswoman holland  what were some of the biggest concerns  well i would say that the biggest  concern about congresswoman deb  holland's nomination for secretary of  interior  is her stance on fossil fuel production  and there was a lot of focus on whether  she as secretary of interior and  president biden  would move to permanently ban oil and  gas drilling and exploration  on federal lands and waters and also on  the issue  of fracking and in fact some republican  senators really came out  to speak strongly against holland's  nomination  uh calling her a radical you know she is  one of the most progressive members of  congress  they also tried to say that she's out of  touch with the west  that's a little bit of a hard you know  thing to prove or to say  seeing as she is a congresswoman from  new mexico  but still they really tried to fight her  nomination because again of the issue  of fossil fuels but congresswoman  holland actually did  you know pretty well during her  confirmation hearings fielding these  questions  and she actually told senators that she  thinks and she understands that fossil  fuels  do have to be a part of the country's  energy  strategy at least for the foreseeable  future  western states had quite a bit of  conflict with the interior department  under the trump administration and ryan  zinke who used to run it  during that administration a lot of  times the questions that are asked in  these hearings kind of reflect  what's important to the states what's  important to what would  happen in the new department of the  interior administration  i think we get a fair idea of this but  what kind of a direction do you  expect or will we expect to see uh in a  in a holland  led interior department the reason that  this is such a historic nomination is  the fact that she is a native american  woman  and so one of the things that i think  people say was lacking  not just during the trump administration  but kind of historically has been  lacking in the department of interior is  a strong representation  of native american tribes and of course  that was alexander limon from washington  d.c  a date for the vote on congresswoman  holland  has not yet been scheduled nevada's  congressional delegation has a new  proposal in washington that could have  some big benefits here in las vegas  it comes from senator cortez masto and  congressman stephen horsford  and it encourages the hospitality injury  industry rather  to re-hire workers and bring convention  business  to do this companies would get a tax  credit to rehire those workers that were  laid off during the pandemic  and travelers would get a credit on all  their trade show related expenses  our first order business of course is  crushing this virus and we are making  great progress in doing that  um and as we defeat this virus we need  to  quickly be able to rebound and this bill  will help us do that  the bill also includes a credit for  restaurants who renovate or increase  their service post pandemic  if this passes in washington all of the  major the measures would be in place for  several years  next on politics now protecting our  great view one of the earliest bills to  pass  a house in the legislature keeps  nevada's rural skies dark  we'll talk about it here on eight news  now  [Music]  welcome back parts of rural nevada have  some of those you see there  darkest skies in the country and a bill  that has been passed by the state senate  aims to keep them  that way senate bill 52 lets the state  give a dark sky designation to  places that don't have a whole lot of  artificial light or light pollution  the goal is to draw in more tourists who  want to bask in the stars  the senate passed the plane unanimously  this week and now heads over to the  state assembly  all right so now in what to watch it's  the return of school here in southern  nevada  monday marks the first day some clark  county school district students  in k through 3rd grade will head back to  actual class  on campus we're going to keep an eye on  how it goes in this first week  and next week also marks a year since  our first coronavirus case here in  nevada  we're going to dig into where we are now  with the pandemic and look back at the  year of lockdowns and  wearing masks all coming up next week  we thank you very much for watching  politics now don't forget you can stay  up to date online on all the things  happening politics here in nevada and  around the country  on eightnewsnow.com we'll see you back  here next saturday take care  [Music]  you
 

